/* ---------- lessons on localization, perception and planning (run in the simulator) ---------- */
// Each try is a tryGo configuration with a level: it reloads that level, applies the settings and runs.
const LV_WAREHOUSE=1,LV_NOISY=2,LV_BUILDING=3,LV_BUSY=4;
const SIM_LESSONS={
loc:[
 {t:'Dead reckoning drifts, and knows it',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every wheeled robot has odometry: encoders on the wheels report how far each one turned, and from that the robot computes its own motion. Between external fixes, which can be a landmark every few seconds or a GPS fix lost for a minute in a tunnel, odometry is all there is. How fast it drifts decides how often the vehicle needs an outside reference. Whether it <i>knows</i> how far it has drifted decides something more important: whether the rest of the stack can act on the uncertainty, slow down, widen its safety margins, or refuse to plan at all. This lesson is about that second question. The first experiment shows a filter whose uncertainty is wrong; the second shows one whose uncertainty is right and useless.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Dead reckoning integrates the odometry through a motion model. Each tick, 50 ms here, the arc model takes the current pose and the odometry input <i>u</i>, the measured speed <i>v</i> and turn rate ω, and moves the pose along a circular arc. Two kinds of error enter. <i>Noise</i> is random from tick to tick: a slightly high reading now, a slightly low one next, partly averaging out but never completely, so the error does a random walk. <i>Bias</i> is systematic: a wheel 2 % larger than the model believes makes the robot overestimate every metre it drives, and no averaging removes that. Nothing in dead reckoning ever corrects either; there is no measurement to compare against.</p>
<p>Heading error is the expensive kind. A speed error of 2 % puts the robot 2 cm off per metre, along the direction of travel. A heading error of 2° puts it 3.5 cm off per metre, sideways, and the sideways error keeps growing with every metre driven after the heading went wrong. That is why the uncertainty ellipse stretches across the direction of travel rather than along it, and why heading sensors (a gyro, a compass) buy so much in practice.</p>
<p><b>The math.</b> Dead reckoning is the prediction half of a Kalman filter run on its own. The state is the pose μ = [<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>, θ] and its covariance Σ, a 3 × 3 matrix whose diagonal holds the variances and whose off-diagonal entries record how the errors move together.</p>
<div class="eq">μ' = g(μ, u)            Σ' = G Σ Gᵀ + V M Vᵀ
σ_x² grows ∝ t from speed noise; a heading error θ turns into position error ∝ v·t·θ, so the ellipse stretches across the direction of travel</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Linearize the motion model about the estimate: a small pose error δ becomes <i>G</i>δ after the step, and the covariance of a linearly transformed error is <i>G</i>Σ<i>G</i>ᵀ; the same argument on the odometry noise gives <i>V</i><i>M</i><i>V</i>ᵀ. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/2-the-motion-model-and-its-jacobians">Derivation: chapter 2 §2</a></p>
<p>The first line moves the estimate. The second moves its uncertainty, in two parts. <i>G</i> Σ <i>G</i>ᵀ carries the uncertainty already there through the motion: <i>G</i> is the Jacobian of the motion model with respect to the pose, and its entries −<i>v</i>Δ<i>t</i> sin θ and <i>v</i>Δ<i>t</i> cos θ are exactly the mechanism above, a heading variance turning into position variance sideways. <i>V</i> <i>M</i> <i>V</i>ᵀ adds this tick's odometry noise, with <i>M</i> = diag(σ<sub>v</sub>², σ<sub>ω</sub>²) and <i>V</i> the Jacobian with respect to the odometry input. Every tick adds, nothing subtracts: Σ grows without bound. In this lab the odometry noise is σ<sub>v</sub> = 0.1 |<i>v</i>| + 0.02 m/s and σ<sub>ω</sub> = 0.2 |ω| + 0.1 |<i>v</i>| + 0.02 rad/s, times the <b>odometry noise</b> slider; the bias is a 2 % speed scale and a turn-rate offset of 0.01 rad/s per m/s, times the <b>calibration bias</b> slider. The filter's <i>M</i> is built from the same noise formula times the <b>assumed motion noise</b> slider. It knows nothing about the bias.</p>
<p>Written out, <i>g</i> is the arc model: it holds <i>v</i> and ω constant for one tick Δ<i>t</i> = 0.05 s, so the pose moves along a circle of radius <i>v</i>/ω, and below |ω| = 10⁻⁶ rad/s the code takes the straight-line limit. The simulator moves the true robot with the same function and the applied (<i>v</i>, ω), with ω = <i>v</i> tan δ/<i>L</i> for the car; the filter feeds it the measured odometry. <i>G</i> and <i>V</i> are the derivatives of the straight-line step, which the filter uses on arcs too: over 50 ms the difference is second order in ωΔ<i>t</i>.</p>
<div class="eq">x' = x + (v/ω)(sin(θ + ωΔt) − sin θ)      ω → 0:  x' = x + vΔt cos θ
y' = y − (v/ω)(cos(θ + ωΔt) − cos θ)              y' = y + vΔt sin θ
θ' = θ + ωΔt
G = [1  0  −vΔt sin θ;  0  1  vΔt cos θ;  0  0  1]     V = [Δt cos θ  0;  Δt sin θ  0;  0  Δt]     M = diag(σ_v², σ_ω²)</div>
<p><b>Reading the result.</b> A filter is called consistent when its covariance matches its real errors: the true error should fall inside the 2σ band about 95 % of the time, and NEES, the squared error measured in units of the filter's own covariance, should average about 3 for three states. The status strip reports NEES over the last 20 s and flags the filter as overconfident above 7.8, the 95 % point of χ² with three degrees of freedom. The Position error plot draws the error against the 2σ band. The band is the filter's honesty, not its accuracy: a consistent filter can be metres off and still be telling the truth about it.</p>`,
  tries:[['Dead reckoning on noisy odometry',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'dr'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'Loads L3 with odometry noise ×1 and calibration bias ×1, sets localization to dead reckoning, opens the Localize tab and the Position error plot, and runs. The map shows the true robot (solid) and the estimate (blue ring) with its 95 % ellipse.',
     see:'The two separate within seconds. After 10 s the estimate is about 1 m off while the band claims 0.6 m; by 40 s it is 2.4 m off against a 1.2 m σ. Over a two-minute run the error is inside the 2σ band only about 60 % of the time, and NEES sits near 10, so the status strip flags the filter as overconfident almost throughout. The ellipse is long across the direction of travel. The consequences are visible on the map: the robot clips shelves it believes are elsewhere, and the mission skips goal after goal because the planner is planning from a pose that does not exist.',
     shows:'The noise model is right about the noise and silent about the bias. Random noise does partly average and is covered by Σ; the 2 % speed bias and the turn-rate offset accumulate in one direction every tick, and nothing in the covariance accounts for them. A filter that models noise but not bias is overconfident by construction. On a real vehicle this is the wheel-radius calibration, tyre pressure, and the load in the back, all of which change the effective wheel size without telling the software.'}],
   ['Noise ×3',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'dr',odoK:3},tab:'veh',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'The same run with the odometry noise slider at ×3 (Vehicle tab). The filter\'s assumed noise follows the slider, so its Σ grows three times faster in standard deviation; the bias is unchanged.',
     see:'The band is now wide: σ is about 1.7 m at 10 s, 6 m by a minute and 10 m by two minutes, about the width of the warehouse. The actual error is similar to the first run, 1 to 2.5 m, because the bias is the same and extra random noise mostly averages. The error now lies inside the band 100 % of the time and NEES falls below 1 by the end: the filter has become underconfident.',
     shows:'Consistency is a ratio, and either side of it can be adjusted. Raising the assumed noise is the cheapest way to make a filter honest, and this run is honest: it really could be anywhere within 10 m. But that honesty buys the planner nothing; a pose known to ±10 m cannot steer down a 2 m aisle. The useful fixes are the ones that shrink the error, not the ones that widen the band: an external measurement (next lesson), or putting the bias into the state so the filter can learn it, which only works once there is a measurement to learn it from.'}]],
  q:[['Why does the ellipse stretch across the direction of travel rather than along it?','Speed noise and speed bias act along the heading, and their effect on position grows with distance driven. Heading noise acts across the heading, and its effect grows with distance driven <i>times</i> the heading error, which itself keeps growing. The sideways term wins after the first few metres, and the <i>G</i> Σ <i>G</i>ᵀ step carries it forward every tick.'],
     ['On L1 the odometry is exact (noise 0, bias 0). What does Σ do there?','It still grows. The filter\'s assumed noise is the slider value with a floor of ×0.25, because a filter cannot know that its odometry is perfect, and a Σ that stopped growing would leave no room for a correction later. Exact odometry with a growing Σ is underconfident, which is the safe direction to be wrong in.'],
     ['What would you add to the state so the filter could learn the 2 % bias?','A scale factor on the speed (and one on the turn rate), with a small process noise so it can drift slowly. Dead reckoning alone cannot estimate it, since nothing distinguishes a biased wheel from a longer trip, but with beacons in the loop the innovations are consistently one-signed and the filter can attribute that to the scale state. Most deployed vehicle filters carry exactly these states.']]},
 {t:'The Kalman update, and NEES as a lie detector',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Fusion is where localization stops drifting. Any sensor that measures something fixed in the world, here the range and bearing to beacons on the walls, can pull the estimate back, and the whole question is how hard to pull: a reading is noisy, the estimate is uncertain, and the right correction weighs one against the other. The Kalman gain is that weighing. It is computed from the covariance, which is why the covariance has to be right: a filter that thinks it is more certain than it is will under-trust its sensors, discount exactly the readings that would correct it, and drift while reporting centimetre accuracy. NEES is the standard test for that failure. It is how real stacks are checked on logged drives with ground truth, and it is a number this lab shows live.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The filter runs a loop of predict, compare, correct. Between readings it predicts with the odometry, exactly as dead reckoning does, and Σ grows. When a beacon reading <i>z</i> arrives it first predicts what the reading <i>should</i> be from the current estimate, <i>ẑ</i>: the range is the distance from the estimated position to the beacon, the bearing is the angle to it minus the estimated heading. The innovation ν = <i>z</i> − <i>ẑ</i> is the part of the reading the filter did not expect. If the filter were exactly right, ν would be pure sensor noise. The update moves the estimate by a fraction of ν, and the fraction is the Kalman gain: large when the estimate is uncertain and the sensor precise, small when the filter is already confident. After the update Σ shrinks, but only in the directions the reading informs. A range measurement constrains the position along the line to the beacon and says nothing across it; a bearing constrains the position across that line and the heading. Several beacons from different directions pin everything down.</p>
<p><b>The math.</b> With <i>H</i> the Jacobian of the measurement model (how the predicted reading changes when the pose changes) and <i>Q</i> the sensor noise covariance:</p>
<div class="eq">S = HΣHᵀ + Q,   K = ΣHᵀS⁻¹
μ ← μ + Kν,   Σ ← (I−KH)Σ(I−KH)ᵀ + KQKᵀ
NEES = eᵀΣ⁻¹e ≈ 3 for a consistent 3-state filter
ẑ = h(μ) = [√q,  atan2(Δy, Δx) − θ],   Δx = m_x − x,  Δy = m_y − y,  q = Δx² + Δy²
H = ∂h/∂x = [−Δx/√q  −Δy/√q  0;  Δy/q  −Δx/q  −1],   Q = diag(σ_r², σ_b²),   ν = z − ẑ with the bearing wrapped to ±π</div>
<p>The last two lines are the measurement model <i>h</i> for one beacon at (<i>m</i><sub>x</sub>, <i>m</i><sub>y</sub>): the range from the estimated position and the bearing relative to the estimated heading, with <i>H</i> its Jacobian. The prediction step between readings is the arc model of the previous lesson.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The error <i>x</i> − μ and the innovation ν are jointly Gaussian (ν ≈ <i>H</i>(<i>x</i> − μ) + noise), so conditioning the error on ν is the standard Gaussian conditioning formula: shift the mean by (cross-covariance)(<i>S</i>)⁻¹ν and subtract the explained covariance. <i>K</i> is that cross-covariance over <i>S</i>; nothing in it is tuned. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/4-the-kalman-update-derived">Derivation: chapter 2 §4</a> · NEES as a χ² variable: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/5-consistency-nees-and-the-2-band">chapter 2 §5</a></p>
<p><i>S</i> is the innovation covariance: how spread out ν should be if the filter is right, the pose uncertainty projected into measurement space plus the sensor noise. <i>K</i> divides the state's uncertainty by that total, so it is literally the ratio "how much of this surprise is my fault". The Σ update is in Joseph form, the long way of writing (<i>I</i> − <i>KH</i>)Σ, which stays symmetric and positive definite in floating point; the short form does not. NEES takes the true error <i>e</i> (available in a simulator, or on a logged drive with ground truth), measures it in units of Σ, and sums over the three states. Each consistent state contributes about 1, so the expected value is 3, and 95 % of samples fall below 7.8. A steady average well above 3 means Σ is too small for the real error: the filter is lying about its confidence.</p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Beacons report every 0.2 s (every fourth tick) within 7 m and in line of sight, with σ<sub>r</sub> = 8 cm and σ<sub>b</sub> = 0.03 rad (about 1.7°). On this level the readings carry known IDs, so there is no association problem yet. The odometry noise and bias are those of the previous lesson, and the <b>assumed motion noise</b> slider in the Localize tab scales the filter\'s <i>M</i>. The status strip shows NEES averaged over the last 20 s and marks it overconfident above 7.8.</p>`,
  tries:[['EKF',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'L3 with the EKF, odometry noise ×1 and bias ×1, assumed noise ×1. Localize tab and the Position error plot.',
     see:'The error stays between 1 and 5 cm for the whole run and the σ reported by the filter is the same size, so NEES hovers between 2.5 and 5 with the error inside the 2σ band about 97 % of the time. All five goals are reached in about 90 s with no collisions, against zero goals for dead reckoning on the same level. Press F (or ◎ on the map) to follow the robot and watch the estimate: between readings it slides with the odometry, and five times a second it snaps toward the truth as a reading lands. The ellipse breathes in the same rhythm, growing for four ticks and collapsing on the fifth.',
     shows:'This is a healthy filter. The small ×1 bias is there, but a beacon fix every 0.2 s corrects what it accumulates in 0.2 s, which is far below the sensor noise, so it never shows. Note what consistency looks like in the plot: the error is not always inside the band; it pokes out about one time in twenty, as it should.'}],
   ['EKF, odometry bias ×3',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf',odoB:3},tab:'veh',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'The same filter with the calibration bias at ×3 (Vehicle tab): the speed reads 6 % high and the turn rate carries an offset of 0.03 rad/s per m/s. The filter is not told.',
     see:'The pose error is still small, 5 to 16 cm, because the beacons keep catching it, but NEES is 8 to 16 whenever the robot is moving, and the status strip flags the filter as overconfident for most of the drive. The error leaves the 2σ band about a third of the time. When the mission completes near 90 s and the robot stops, NEES falls back to 2 to 5 within a few seconds.',
     shows:'The classic signature of unmodeled bias. Σ shrinks as if the odometry were unbiased, so the gain discounts the innovations, which are no longer zero-mean noise but a steady, one-signed pull (the filter keeps believing it went a little farther and turned a little more than it did). The error is small only because the beacons are frequent and precise. The same bias with a landmark every 50 m instead of every 0.2 s would be metres, with the filter still claiming centimetres. NEES drops when the robot stops because the bias is proportional to speed: no motion, no bias, and the filter is suddenly telling the truth again.'}],
   ['Bias ×3, assumed noise ×4',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf',odoB:3,filtK:4},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'Bias ×3 as before, and the filter\'s assumed motion noise at ×4 (Localize tab), so its uncertainty grows four times faster between readings.',
     see:'NEES comes down to 2 to 3 while driving, the error is inside the band 99 % of the time, and the pose error is actually a little smaller than in the previous run, 1 to 4 cm instead of 5 to 16. The ellipse is visibly larger between readings and collapses harder at each one.',
     shows:'Inflating the process noise is the standard practical fix, and it is what "tuning <i>Q</i>" means in most deployed filters: the extra assumed noise stands in for the bias the model lacks. It works here because it makes Σ large enough between readings that the gain trusts the beacons more, which is also why the error improves. The cost comes later: a filter that admits more ignorance between fixes will lose confidence faster during a beacon outage (correct, but the planner has to live with it) and will accept readings from farther away when it starts gating them, which is the next lesson\'s problem. A bias state in the filter would fix the cause instead of the symptom.'}]],
  q:[['Why is the expected NEES 3, and not 1 or 9?','Σ⁻¹ whitens the error: if the filter is consistent, Σ^{−1/2} <i>e</i> has three components that are each standard normal, and the sum of three squared standard normals is χ² with three degrees of freedom, whose mean is 3. With a 2-D position-only check it would be 2; for a track with position and velocity, 4.'],
     ['Overconfident and underconfident are both inconsistent. Which is more dangerous downstream?','Overconfident. An overconfident filter rejects or discounts the measurements that would correct it, so the error grows while the reported uncertainty does not; the planner then plans with margins that are too small around a pose that is wrong. An underconfident filter wastes information and makes the planner over-cautious, which is a performance cost, not a safety one. Most practitioners tune toward slight underconfidence for that reason.'],
     ['In the second experiment the error stayed under 16 cm. Why worry about NEES at all if the pose is that good?','Because the pose is good for a reason that will not hold: beacons every 0.2 s. NEES is measuring the filter, not the scenario. The same filter in a scenario with sparser fixes, a longer outage, or a wrong association will fail, and NEES on a benign log is the warning you get before it does.']]},
 {t:'Gating, and the kidnapped robot',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every localization system that works from unlabeled features has to decide which feature each reading came from, and every one that works outdoors has to survive readings that came from nothing at all: reflections, other vehicles, a bird. The gate is the standard answer to both, and it is a good one, but it has a failure mode that is easy to miss on the bench and expensive in the field. A filter that is badly wrong, for whatever reason, sees every reading as an outlier, throws them all away, and stays wrong with full confidence. The kidnapping in this lesson is the cleanest way to produce that state on demand; in practice it is produced by a long sensor outage, a wheel slipping on a ramp, or a map that changed.</p>
<p><b>The experiment.</b> Each button restarts L3, drives for 8 s, then picks the robot up and puts it down at a free spot at least 6 m away (usually more than 12 m) without telling the estimator. That is a kidnapping. The run continues from just after the jump. On the map, compare the solid disc (where the robot really is) with the blue ring (where the filter thinks it is) and the pink ellipse or the particles (how sure it is). The Position error plot and the pose error in the status strip jump to about 15 m at 8 s. The question is whether they come back down.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> With unlabeled beacons a reading does not say which beacon it came from. The filter predicts what each candidate beacon would read from the current estimate, takes the candidate whose prediction is closest, and uses the reading only if it is close enough. "Close" has to be measured in the right units: a 0.3 m range error is nothing when the filter is uncertain by a metre and damning when it is sure to a centimetre. So the distance is the innovation divided by its own expected spread, the Mahalanobis distance, and the threshold is a probability, not a number of metres.</p>
<div class="eq">d² = νᵀ S⁻¹ ν < 9.21   (χ² with 2 degrees of freedom, 99 %)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> If the candidate is the right beacon and the filter is consistent, ν is zero-mean Gaussian with covariance <i>S</i>, so <i>d</i>² is the squared length of a 2-D standard normal vector: χ² with two degrees of freedom, whose 99 % point is 9.21. A correct reading is rejected 1 % of the time; that is the price. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/6-data-association-and-the-gate">Derivation: chapter 2 §6</a></p>
<p><b>What the gate does after a kidnapping.</b> Every reading is now tens of σ from its prediction, every <i>d</i>² is in the thousands, every reading is rejected. The filter keeps predicting with the odometry alone. Its Σ grows only at the slow odometry rate, so the gate stays narrow for minutes, and the filter discards exactly the evidence that would correct it. It keeps its small ellipse and stays confidently wrong. The status strip's NEES is the tell: it reads in the tens of thousands, which is what a 15 m error measured in centimetres of claimed σ looks like, and it stays there because the error never shrinks.</p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The kidnap is a teleport to a random free cell; the robot's speed is zeroed so it starts again from rest. The estimators are not reset. On this level the lost guard is off, so the mission keeps driving on the wrong estimate; the planner notices only that the robot is "off path" or "stuck" and replans from the wrong pose. The NEES average covers the last 20 s, so after a jump it reports enormous values for 20 s even for a filter that recovers in 5.</p>`,
  tries:[['1 · EKF, known IDs',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos',act:'kidnap'},
    {setup:'L3, EKF, readings carry their beacon ID, so there is no association step and no gate. Kidnapped at 8 s.',
     see:'The jump is about 16 m. One second later the error is 6.6 m, after three seconds 2.6 m, after seven 0.5 m, and from 14 s on it is back under 10 cm; the first goal is reached around 19 s. The recovery is not a snap but a geometric decay: each reading removes roughly a third of what is left. Meanwhile the event log fills with "Replan (off path)" as the planner chases the moving estimate. NEES shows tens of thousands until the 20 s window has forgotten the jump.',
     shows:'With the correct beacon named, the innovation is huge but honest, and the update <i>K</i>ν drags the estimate toward the truth. It drags rather than jumps because the gain is small: Σ is a few centimetres, so the filter believes its own position far more than any single reading and moves only a fraction of the way each time. A filter that could inflate Σ when its innovations are consistently large would recover in one or two readings; that is what the particle filter\'s injection test does, in the third experiment.'}],
   ['2 · EKF, unlabeled',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf',assoc:'unknown'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos',act:'kidnap'},
    {setup:'The same filter with unlabeled beacons: nearest-neighbour association and the 99 % gate.',
     see:'The error stays at 16 m for the whole run. The ellipse stays small (σ about 8 cm, creeping to 30 cm after a minute). The Gated measurements counter in the status strip climbs by about 15 per second: every reading fails the gate. The robot, steering by an estimate that is in a different aisle, hits a shelf within a few seconds and then spends the run being "stuck", replanning, and skipping goals one after another; after 90 s it has reached none.',
     shows:'The gate is doing its job as designed and the design has no exit. The readings that would correct the filter are the ones it rejects, and they will go on being rejected until Σ has grown enough to admit them, which at the odometry rate takes minutes, and by then the nearest beacon will often be the wrong one. A gated Gaussian filter needs an external trigger to say "you are lost, start over"; it cannot find out on its own from readings it refuses to look at.'}],
   ['3 · Particle filter, unlabeled',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'pf',assoc:'unknown'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos',act:'kidnap'},
    {setup:'The particle filter with 400 particles, unlabeled beacons, injection on. Kidnapped at 8 s.',
     see:'The jump is about 15 m. Inside the filter the fast likelihood average falls from about 0.3 to 0.1 within two seconds while the slow one stays near 0.12; in the status strip <i>N</i><sub>eff</sub> collapses to about 15 of 400 and the injection fraction rises to its 25 % cap. New particles appear at the ranges the readings report; by 11 s the error is 16 cm. Around 14 s the set briefly splits into two clusters (σ jumps to 6 m for two seconds) and then settles on the true one; from 16 s the error is a few centimetres and the first goal is reached at 24 s.',
     shows:'Three things the EKF cannot do. The particle set holds several hypotheses at once, and each particle does its own association, so a particle near the true pose finds the beacons that fit it even while the main cluster does not. The filter watches whether the readings fit at all, through the fast and slow likelihood averages, which is a kidnap detector built from the same innovations the EKF throws away. And it can put probability mass anywhere the readings allow, by placing new particles at the measured range from a beacon, so the belief can move 15 m in one update without any Σ having to grow first. The split at 14 s is the filter being honest that two places fit the readings for a moment.'}]],
  q:[['The gate rejects a correct reading 1 % of the time. What would a 99.99 % gate (d² < 18.4) change?','Fewer good readings lost, more clutter admitted, and no difference at all after a kidnapping: the innovations there are hundreds of σ, far beyond any gate anyone would set. The gate is a tool for the normal case; recovery from gross error needs a separate mechanism.'],
     ['Why does the known-ID EKF recover geometrically rather than in one update?','The gain is Σ<i>H</i>ᵀ<i>S</i>⁻¹, and Σ is a few centimetres while the innovation is metres. The filter trusts itself more than any one reading, so each update moves a fixed fraction of the remaining error. It would recover in one step only if Σ were large, which it is not, because nothing told the filter that its uncertainty had changed.'],
     ['Could an EKF detect its own kidnapping?','Yes, from the statistics of its innovations: a run of consecutive rejections, or a normalized innovation squared that stays far above its χ² expectation, is exactly the signal the particle filter\'s fast and slow averages read. A practical EKF stack monitors that and re-initializes from the readings when it trips, which is global localization by another name.']]},
 {t:'Particles: resampling, injection and symmetry',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A Gaussian filter can hold one hypothesis. A robot switched on in an unknown place, or recovering from being lost, needs to hold many, and to let the readings kill them off one by one. The particle filter is the standard way to do that, and it is also the most honest estimator here about what it does not know, which is why its output drives the lost guard on the busy floor. Its failure modes are different from the EKF\'s: it does not get stuck confidently wrong, but it can collapse onto a lucky particle, run out of diversity, or settle on a pose that is indistinguishable from the right one.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Represent the belief by 400 sample poses, the particles, each with a weight. Prediction moves every particle through the motion model with its own random odometry noise, so the cloud spreads the way Σ would grow. Correction multiplies each weight by how likely the reading is from that particle\'s pose, so particles that explain the reading well gain weight and the rest fade. Left alone the weights collapse onto one particle, so the filter resamples: it draws a new set of 400 from the old one in proportion to weight, copying the heavy ones and dropping the light ones. The estimate shown on the map is the weighted mean, and the reported Σ is the weighted scatter, which is why it is honestly enormous when the cloud is spread over the floor.</p>
<div class="eq">N_eff = 1 / Σ w_i²        resample when N_eff < N/2
augmented MCL: w_fast ← w_fast + 0.1 (w − w_fast),  w_slow ← w_slow + 0.001 (w − w_slow)
inject up to 25 % new particles with probability max(0, 1 − w_fast / w_slow)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Weighting by the likelihood is Bayes\' rule applied one sample at a time; the weighted sum of any function of the particles approximates its expectation under the posterior. <i>N</i><sub>eff</sub> is the number of equally weighted particles that would carry the same statistical information as the current unequal set, which is why resampling is triggered on it and not on a timer. Low-variance resampling walks the cumulative weights with one random offset, so a particle of weight <i>w</i> is copied ⌊<i>Nw</i>⌋ or ⌈<i>Nw</i>⌉ times, never wildly more. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/7-the-particle-filter">Derivation: chapter 2 §7</a></p>
<p><b>The models.</b> Each particle <i>i</i> moves by the arc model <i>g</i> of the dead-reckoning lesson with its own draw of odometry noise (σ<sub>v</sub>, σ<sub>ω</sub> from the same noise formula, at least 0.02). A beacon reading (<i>r</i>, <i>b</i>) is scored by a Gaussian on its range and bearing as predicted from that particle (<i>r̂</i>, <i>b̂</i>), taking the best-fitting beacon <i>j</i> when they are unlabeled, plus a floor of 10⁻³. The lidar adds a likelihood field: <i>d</i> is the distance from a beam's end point, placed from the particle, to the nearest wall of the map. An injected particle is placed by inverting one reading with a random direction φ.</p>
<div class="eq">predict:  x_i ← g(x_i, (v + σ_v ε₁, ω + σ_ω ε₂)),   ε₁, ε₂ ~ N(0, 1)
beacons:  log w_i += Σ_readings log( max_j exp(−½[(r − r̂_j)²/σ_r² + (b − b̂_j)²/σ_b²]) + 10⁻³ )
lidar:    log w_i += 0.4 · Σ_24 beams log( 0.9 exp(−d²/(2·0.2²)) + 0.05 )
inject:   (x, y) = m_j − (r + σ_r ε)·(cos φ, sin φ),   θ = φ − (b + σ_b ε),   φ uniform</div>
<p><b>Injection.</b> The filter keeps a slow and a fast running average of how well the readings fit the particle set. When the fast one drops well below the slow one, the readings have stopped fitting: the robot was moved, or the cloud converged on the wrong place. The filter then replaces up to a quarter of the particles with new ones, each placed where one current reading says the robot could be: at the measured range from a beacon, facing so that the bearing matches. That is what makes global localization possible at all. Scatter the particles over the whole floor and the filter still converges, because the readings keep proposing places and the next readings keep testing them.</p>
<p><b>Symmetry.</b> The warehouse is nearly the same after a 180° turn about its centre. With unlabeled beacons the readings cannot tell the true pose from the mirror pose, so the cloud may converge on either. A labeled beacon breaks the tie at once. The lidar against the map breaks it too, but slowly: the map\'s asymmetries are small and visible only from some places, and the lidar likelihood is deliberately tempered so that 24 beams cannot collapse the set on their own.</p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> L5 is the busy floor: unlabeled beacons, clutter, the lidar on, and the lost guard on, so when σ exceeds 1 m the mission stops and the robot turns in place until σ is back under 0.4 m. Each button restarts the level, drives 8 s, then scatters all 400 particles uniformly over free space, which is global localization from nothing. The particle cloud, the mean estimate and its ellipse are drawn on the map; the status strip shows <i>N</i><sub>eff</sub> and the injection fraction live (the fast and slow averages are internal).</p>`,
  tries:[['Scatter the particles (unlabeled)',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{loc:'pf'},tab:'loc',act:'scatter'},
    {setup:'L5 defaults: unlabeled beacons, lidar on, injection on. Scattered at 8 s.',
     see:'The cloud covers the floor (σ about 8.5 m) for one update, then collapses within a second onto a tight cluster, confident to 2 cm, on the mirror pose: 14 m from the truth with the heading 180° off. The lost guard releases the mission and the robot drives on the wrong belief. Over the next minute the fast average sags, the guard trips a few times (σ leaps, up to 25 % of the particles are injected, the robot turns in place) and the set returns to the mirror each time. Around 85 s the readings finally favour the true pose, the cloud jumps across the floor, and from then on the error is centimetres; the first goal falls at about 110 s.',
     shows:'Convergence is not correctness. The filter did exactly what the readings justified: with unlabeled beacons the two poses are equally likely, and it picked one. What eventually broke the tie was the lidar seeing an asymmetry of the map from the right place. The long wait is the honest cost of a symmetric environment and an unlabeled sensor; a real stack would add a second cue (a labeled landmark, a magnetometer, a WiFi fingerprint) rather than wait.'}],
   ['Scatter with known IDs',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{loc:'pf',assoc:'known'},tab:'loc',act:'scatter'},
    {setup:'The same scatter with readings that name their beacon.',
     see:'The lost guard holds the robot for three seconds while the cloud sorts itself out: 7 m error after one update, 0.7 m after three, 16 cm after four. The mission resumes at about 12 s and the first goal is reached around 38 s, with the error at a few centimetres throughout.',
     shows:'With the symmetry removed, three or four readings are enough to pick out the true pose from 400 scattered guesses, because each reading now rules out every particle that is at the wrong range or bearing from a specific beacon. The lesson of the pair is that labels are worth more than particles: identity is the cheapest disambiguation there is.'}],
   ['Scatter, lidar off',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{loc:'pf',pfLidar:false},tab:'loc',act:'scatter'},
    {setup:'Unlabeled beacons and no lidar term in the weights; only the beacon likelihood is left.',
     see:'The cloud collapses onto a wrong cluster at once (near the centre of the floor the mirror pose is close in position but 165° off in heading), loses it, and spends the next hundred seconds in the LOST state with σ around 7 m, the robot turning in place and the fast average never settling. Near 118 s it converges on the truth and reaches a goal at about 150 s; then at 160 s the set collapses again (<i>N</i><sub>eff</sub> down to 3–9) and the robot is lost once more.',
     shows:'Without a second sensor the beacon readings alone cannot separate the two poses, and without the lidar\'s steady evidence the set keeps being pulled apart by clutter and injection. The filter is right to be unsure. Watch what the planner does with a belief like that: nothing, which is the lost guard working as intended.'}]],
  q:[['Why 400 particles, and not 40 or 40 000?','Global localization has to cover a three-dimensional pose space finely enough that some particle lands near the truth; with injection proposing poses from the readings, 400 is plenty here. Tracking a converged filter would need far fewer. The cost is linear in <i>N</i> per update, and 40 000 particles would spend their time resampling copies of the same few poses.'],
     ['Why is the lidar likelihood multiplied by 0.4 before it enters the weights?','Twenty-four beams treated as independent evidence give a likelihood so sharply peaked that one particle wins every update and the set collapses onto its sampling noise. The beams are not independent (neighbouring beams hit the same wall), so tempering is a crude correction for over-counting. It is the particle filter\'s version of keeping Σ honest.'],
     ['When the set holds two clusters, the mean lies between them. What should the planner do with that?','Nothing; the mean is in a place the robot is certainly not. The reported Σ is enormous in that case, which is the right signal, and the lost guard reads it to stop the mission and turn in place until one cluster wins. An estimator that reported the biggest cluster instead would be more useful and less honest.']]},
 {t:'EKF-SLAM: the map is part of the state',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Everything so far assumed the beacon positions were known. A robot in a new building has no map; a vehicle whose map is six months old has the wrong one. Simultaneous localization and mapping estimates the map and the pose together, and the mechanism that makes it work, the correlation between the robot\'s error and the errors of everything it has mapped, is also what makes it expensive and what makes loop closure feel like magic the first time you see a whole map shift at once.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Append the beacon positions to the state. The first time a beacon is seen it is placed from the current pose and the reading, so its error is the pose error plus sensor noise, and it stays correlated with the pose. Every later reading of that beacon constrains the pose <i>relative to the landmark</i>, never absolutely: nothing in a range-and-bearing reading says where either of them is in the world. So the absolute error of the whole structure, robot and map together, drifts like dead reckoning between re-observations of old landmarks. When the robot sees again a beacon it mapped while it was still certain, the correction is applied through the correlations to the pose and to every landmark placed since: that is loop closure.</p>
<div class="eq">x = [x, y, θ, m₁ₓ, m₁ᵧ, …],   P is (3 + 2n)²
new landmark:  m = p + r·[cos(θ+b), sin(θ+b)],   P_mm = J_x P_xx J_xᵀ + J_z R J_zᵀ
J_x = [1  0  −r sin(θ+b);  0  1  r cos(θ+b)]     J_z = [cos(θ+b)  −r sin(θ+b);  sin(θ+b)  r cos(θ+b)]     R = diag(σ_r², σ_b²)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The landmark initialization is the same linearization argument as the motion model: the new landmark is a function of the pose and the reading, so its covariance is the pose covariance pushed through <i>J</i><sub>x</sub> plus the sensor noise pushed through <i>J</i><sub>z</sub>, and its cross-covariance with the pose is <i>J</i><sub>x</sub><i>P</i><sub>xx</sub>. The measurement Jacobian\'s landmark columns are the negatives of its pose columns, which is the algebraic form of "only relative positions are observed". <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/8-ekf-slam-the-map-joins-the-state">Derivation: chapter 2 §8</a></p>
<p>Prediction and update are the EKF's on the larger state. The pose moves by the arc model <i>g</i> with the Jacobians <i>G</i>, <i>V</i> of the dead-reckoning lesson and the landmarks stay put; a reading of landmark <i>j</i> is predicted from the difference <i>m</i><sub>j</sub> − <i>p</i>, so its Jacobian <i>H</i><sub>j</sub> is the beacon Jacobian of the Kalman lesson on the pose columns and the negative of its position part on that landmark's columns.</p>
<div class="eq">predict:  (x, y, θ) ← g(x, y, θ, u),   P_xx ← G P_xx Gᵀ + V M Vᵀ,   P_xm ← G P_xm,   P_mm unchanged
update:   ẑ_j = [√q,  atan2(Δy, Δx) − θ],   (Δx, Δy) = m_j − p,   ∂h/∂m_j = −∂h/∂p
          K = P H_jᵀ S⁻¹,   x ← x + Kν,   P ← P − K S Kᵀ  (then symmetrized)</div>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Readings carry their beacon ID on this level, so association is free; a beacon seen for the first time is added to the state. Prediction updates only the pose block and its cross terms; updates use the full (3 + 2<i>n</i>)² covariance. The map ellipses drawn on the beacons are the landmarks\' own covariances. The beacon range slider sets how far the robot sees: with 7 m few beacons are visible from any one place and the map is a chain of weakly connected pieces; with 12 m most of the floor shares landmarks and the map closes on itself continuously.</p>`,
  tries:[['EKF-SLAM, range 7 m',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'slam'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'L3 with EKF-SLAM and the default 7 m beacon range; the filter starts with an empty map.',
     see:'The error grows to about 0.5 m in the first 10 s and 1.4 m by 20 s, then plateaus between 1.1 and 1.4 m for the rest of the run instead of growing further. The filter\'s σ settles near 0.55 m, so NEES sits around 8 and the status strip calls it overconfident. The mission suffers: one goal reached in 150 s, four skipped as "stuck", two collisions. The map ellipses on the beacons stay large.',
     shows:'The plateau is SLAM working and its limit at the same time. Re-observing the same few beacons keeps the pose bounded <i>relative to them</i>, but the map itself was laid down from an already-drifted pose and nothing ties it to the world, so the whole structure is offset by a metre and stays there. The overconfidence has the same cause as in lesson 2, the unmodeled odometry bias, now baked into the landmark positions as well.'}],
   ['EKF-SLAM, range 12 m',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'slam',lmRange:12},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'The same filter with beacons visible to 12 m.',
     see:'The error rises to about 1 m by 40 s, then falls: 0.4 m at 60 s, 0.3 m at 90 s, 7 cm from 100 s on, with NEES between 1 and 3.6 throughout. Four of five goals are reached. Watch the map ellipses shrink together when the robot returns toward beacons it mapped at the start.',
     shows:'With most of the floor sharing landmarks, loop closures happen all the time rather than once. Each re-observation of an early landmark corrects the pose and, through the correlations, every landmark placed since, which is why the error falls after rising rather than plateauing. The longer range costs nothing in the filter except larger matrices; what it buys is a connected map.'}],
   ['EKF with the known map',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf'},tab:'loc',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'The plain EKF of lesson 2 on the same level, with the beacon positions given.',
     see:'Error between 1 and 5 cm for the whole run, five goals in about 90 s, no collisions.',
     shows:'This is the price of the map. Between 5 cm and 1.4 m lies the difference between measuring the robot against the world and measuring it against things it placed itself. It is also why mapping is usually done once, carefully, with loop closures forced, and localization against that map is what runs in the vehicle.'}]],
  q:[['Why does SLAM\'s absolute error never reach zero even when every loop is closed?','Because no reading says where anything is in the world; only relative positions are observed. The frame is pinned by the initial pose, so the whole map carries the initial uncertainty plus whatever drift occurred before the first loop closed. That is the gauge freedom of SLAM, and it is why real systems anchor the map with GPS or a surveyed landmark when they need world coordinates.'],
     ['The covariance <i>P</i> becomes dense: every landmark correlated with every other. Is that waste?','No, it is the information. The correlations are how a correction to one landmark propagates to the others, and dropping them (keeping only the diagonal) is known to make EKF-SLAM inconsistent and eventually wrong. The cost is <i>O</i>(<i>n</i>²) per update, which is why systems with thousands of features moved to sparse graph formulations that keep the same information in a different shape.'],
     ['What breaks if the beacons were unlabeled in SLAM?','Association against a map that is itself uncertain: a reading from a new beacon can look like a reading from a mapped one that has drifted, and vice versa. The level\'s own Try list under the level bar has an "EKF-SLAM, unlabeled" button that shows the result. Wrong associations in SLAM are worse than in localization because they corrupt the map, which then corrupts every later pose.']]}],
per:[
 {t:'Log-odds mapping',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A robot that has to work in a building it has not seen must build its own map while driving, and everything downstream, the costmap, the planner, the people detector, reads that map as if it were true. Two decisions shape it: how to combine thousands of noisy beams per second into a belief per cell without the cost exploding, and whose coordinates to draw it in. The first has an elegant answer, log-odds. The second has no answer at all: the map is drawn in the frame of the pose estimate, so every localization error becomes a map error, permanently. This lesson shows both.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Cut the floor into 0.1 m cells and ask of each one: occupied or not? Each lidar beam is a small piece of evidence. The cells it crosses are probably free (the beam went through them), the cell it ends in is probably occupied (something stopped it). Bayes' rule says how to combine evidence, but applying it as probabilities means a multiply and a renormalization per cell per beam. Written in log-odds, the logarithm of the probability ratio occupied to free, the same update is one addition: evidence simply adds up, and a cell seen a thousand times costs no more than one seen once. A cap on the log-odds keeps any cell from becoming so certain that it can never be corrected when the pallet moves.</p>
<div class="eq">l ← clamp(l + l_meas, ±4),   l_free = −0.4,   l_hit = +0.85
occupied if l > 0.5;   unknown while l ≈ 0
l = ln(p / (1 − p)),   p = 1 / (1 + e^(−l))
inverse sensor model:  l_meas = l_hit for the cell a returned beam ends in,  l_free for every other cell it crosses
                       (a beam at maximum range adds l_free to all of its cells)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Apply Bayes' rule to the cell being occupied and to it being free, divide the two, and the normalizer cancels; take the logarithm and the product of evidence becomes a sum. With a 50 % prior the prior term is zero and the update is just <i>l</i> + <i>l</i><sub>meas</sub>. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/2-occupancy-mapping-as-a-binary-bayes-filter">Derivation: chapter 3 §2</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The lidar has 120 beams over 360°, 8 m range, 10 Hz, 2 cm noise. Every scan, each beam is traced cell by cell from the robot to its end point with Bresenham's algorithm and the cells get −0.4 (crossed) or +0.85 (end, if the beam returned); the values correspond to "40 % occupied" and "70 % occupied", so one hit is enough to call a cell occupied and it takes about two clean passes to undo it. The cap of ±4 is 98 %. The costmap is rebuilt from the log-odds every 0.5 s and the planner replans whenever a newly occupied cell lands on its path. The map is drawn in the <i>estimated</i> frame, from the pose the EKF with beacons reports on this level; the true map is shown faintly underneath as a ghost so you can compare.</p>`,
  tries:[['Map the building (EKF)',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,tab:'per'},
    {setup:'L4, the unknown building, with the EKF on beacons for localization and mapping on. Perceive tab; the map layer shows the log-odds grid, dark for occupied, light for free, grey for unseen.',
     see:'The map grows outward from the robot as it drives, walls appearing as thin dark lines within a scan or two of coming into range. Each newly discovered wall across the planned path triggers a "Replan (path blocked)" in the event log, three in this run, and the mission reaches all four goals in about 80 s with the pose error under 5 cm throughout. Measured against the truth, 100 % of the cells the map calls occupied have a real obstacle within one cell, and 96 % of the obstacle cells the lidar has seen are in the map.',
     shows:'With a good pose the log-odds map is almost exact, and it is cheap: 1200 beam traces per second on a 38 400-cell grid. Note how walls thicken slightly where the robot saw them from several places; that is the discretization, not the sensor. Watch the ghost: everywhere the robot has been, the two agree.'}],
   ['Map with dead reckoning',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,set:{loc:'dr'},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'The same level with localization switched to dead reckoning (odometry noise ×1, bias ×0.5 on this level).',
     see:'The map and the ghost separate as the pose drifts: 1.4 m off by 40 s, 3.8 m by 80 s. Walls are painted as parallel ghosts of themselves, later scans erase parts of earlier walls, and the map ends up with fragments that do not exist and gaps where walls do. Only 23 % of the mapped occupied cells are near a real obstacle, and only 18 % of the seen obstacle cells are mapped. The planner replans ten times, reports "no path to goal 3" three times in a row from a costmap full of phantom walls, skips two of the four goals, and the run ends with two reached.',
     shows:'The map does not degrade gracefully with pose error; it fills with contradictions, because every scan is drawn in a different wrong frame. A planner then has to route around walls that are not there, and may be unable to route through a doorway that is. This is why SLAM (chapter 2 §8) exists: if the map must be built, the pose must be estimated against it, not alongside it.'}],
   ['Hide the true-map ghost',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,layers:{ghost:false},tab:'view'},
    {setup:'The EKF run again with the ghost layer off (Layers tab), so the map shows only what the robot believes.',
     see:'The same run as the first experiment, without the answer key: grey unknown space, walls that end where the lidar stopped seeing them, rooms that do not exist yet.',
     shows:'This is what the planner sees. Everything it decides about unseen rooms comes from the unknown-cell policy of the next lesson, not from the map.'}]],
  q:[['Why use log-odds instead of storing a probability per cell?','Because Bayes\' rule for a binary cell becomes an addition in log-odds, with no normalization, and the clamp is a simple cap. Storing probabilities would need a multiply, a renormalize and care near 0 and 1 for every one of the thousand beams per second. Log-odds also make the asymmetry explicit: +0.85 for a hit against −0.4 for a crossing is a design choice about which error is cheaper.'],
     ['A person stands still in a corridor for ten seconds. What happens to the map and the tracker?','Their hits land in the same cells for a hundred scans and the cells saturate at +4: the person becomes a wall. The people detector only looks at hits the map cannot explain, so the track is dropped. When they walk away, the cells need about ten free beams each to be erased, and until then the planner routes around an obstacle that has left. Real stacks keep a separate short-memory layer for dynamic obstacles for exactly this reason.'],
     ['Could the map be built in the true frame instead?','Only in a simulator. On a real robot there is no true frame, there is the estimate, and the map inherits whatever error it has. The honest options are to make the estimate good (beacons, loop closure) or to estimate the pose against the map as it is built (SLAM), so that at least map and pose are consistent with each other even when both are off.']]},
 {t:'Costmap: lethal cells and the inflation band',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A planner does not see walls; it sees a cost for every cell it might enter. That translation, from a map to a cost, is where a robot\'s size, its margin for error and its taste for the middle of corridors are encoded. Get it wrong one way and the robot scrapes shelves; the other way and it refuses doorways it fits through, or plans nowhere at all because everything it has not seen is treated as a wall.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> First, distance: every cell needs to know how far the nearest obstacle is. A distance transform computes that for the whole grid in two sweeps. Then two thresholds. Cells closer than the robot\'s radius plus a margin are lethal: the body would overlap something there, no matter the cost. Beyond that, a penalty that fades out across the inflation band makes the planner prefer the middle of a corridor without forbidding the sides; it will enter the band when the detour would cost more. Finally, a policy for the cells the map has never seen: free, which plans optimistically into unexplored rooms and corrects as walls appear, or lethal, which is cautious and cannot plan anywhere new on its own.</p>
<div class="eq">lethal if d < 0.4 m (radius 0.3 + margin)
penalty = ((0.4 + w − d)/w)²  for d < 0.4 + w,   edge cost = length · (1 + 3 · penalty)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The distance transform is a two-pass chamfer: sweep forward taking the minimum over already-visited neighbours with weights 1 and √2, then sweep backward; the result is the shortest 8-connected distance, within 8 % of the Euclidean one and never smaller. The quadratic penalty is a choice, not a derivation: a cell at the inner edge costs four times its length, at the outer edge nothing, and the curvature in between means brushing the band is cheap and hugging the lethal boundary is not. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/3-the-costmap-distance-transform-and-inflation">Derivation: chapter 3 §3</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Robot radius 0.3 m, margin 0.1 m, so lethal below 0.4 m. The band width is the inflation slider (0.6 m by default), and the penalty multiplier is 3. The costmap is recomputed every 0.5 s from the log-odds map (or from the true map on levels with a known map). The cost layer draws lethal cells dark and the band as a fading tint.</p>`,
  tries:[['Show the cost layer',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,layers:{cost:true},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'L2, the warehouse with a known map, with the cost layer on. Default band of 0.6 m.',
     see:'Every shelf and wall wears a dark lethal collar 0.4 m wide and a tinted band 0.6 m beyond it; the aisles have a clear channel down the middle. The planned path runs along those channels and cuts into the band only at corners. The mission takes four A* plans of 8.6, 13.0, 12.9 and 26.1 m and the robot never comes closer than 0.27 m to a shelf.',
     shows:'The costmap is the planner\'s whole view of the world: it will trade a longer route for a cheaper one by exactly the factor the band sets. Everything about the robot\'s shape has been reduced to one radius and one margin; the car-like robot on L6 needs three circles for the same purpose.'}],
   ['Inflation 1.4 m',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{infl:1.4},layers:{cost:true},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'The same level with the band widened to 1.4 m, which is wider than most aisles are from their centre line.',
     see:'The tint now fills the aisles edge to edge and the planner replans at once ("costmap changed"). The four legs become 9.1, 14.2, 13.3 and 27.6 m, 6 % longer in total, and the search expands about twice as many nodes. The path hugs the centre of open floor more tightly, but the closest approach to a shelf in this run is 0.20 m, no better than before.',
     shows:'A band wider than the corridor is symmetric: every route through the corridor pays the same penalty, so the band stops steering and only adds cost. The closest approach is set by the tracker cutting a corner in a doorway, which no band changes, only the lethal radius does. The band is a preference about open space; margins against contact belong in the lethal threshold.'}],
   ['Unknown = lethal',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,set:{unknownAs:'lethal'},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'L4, the unknown building, with unseen cells treated as obstacles.',
     see:'The robot does not move. All four goals lie in unseen space, so every plan fails with "goal is inside an inflated obstacle", three attempts each, and the mission skips the last goal 17 s in. The map stays a small island around the start.',
     shows:'Cautious and useless on its own: a planner that cannot enter the unknown cannot discover anything. The missing piece is frontier exploration, a behaviour that picks goals on the boundary between seen and unseen space and drives there, which turns the cautious policy into a safe one. Optimistic planning gets the same job done by replanning every time it is wrong, which on this level is three times in 80 s.'}]],
  q:[['Why is the lethal radius 0.4 m and not 0.3 m, the robot\'s radius?','The extra 0.1 m is the budget for everything the planner does not model: tracking error, the 0.1 m cell size, the chamfer transform\'s rounding. A planned path grazing a 0.3 m boundary would put the real robot in contact every time the tracker was a few centimetres off, which is always.'],
     ['The band is 0.6 m and an aisle is 2 m wide. Where does the cheapest straight path run?','Down the middle, 1 m from each side, where the distance is 1.0 m and the penalty is zero on both sides. Offset the path by 0.3 m and one side enters the band at d = 0.7 m with penalty ((1.0 − 0.7)/0.6)² = 0.25, a 75 % surcharge on those cells. The quadratic makes the centre a flat valley with steep walls.'],
     ['The cost layer is rebuilt every 0.5 s. Why not every scan?','A full distance transform over 38 400 cells costs a few milliseconds, which is affordable at 2 Hz and wasteful at 10 Hz, and the planner only replans when its path crosses a newly lethal cell. The map itself updates every scan; it is the derived cost that is cached.']]},
 {t:'Tracking people: from lidar hits to tracks',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Everything that moves has to be found, followed and predicted, because the planner needs where a person will be, not where their hits are now. On a real robot there is no ground truth to compare against, only this chain: detection (which hits are not the map), association (which detection is which person), tracking (a filter per person) and prediction. Each link has a classic failure, and the one that matters most for a speed planner is velocity: it is never measured, only inferred, so it is late and noisy, and the prediction built on it points the wrong way for a second after every change of pace.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> A person is a cluster of lidar hits that the static map cannot explain. Each confirmed person gets a constant-velocity Kalman filter: the state is position and velocity, the prediction moves the position by the velocity, and the only measurement is a position. Detections are matched to predicted tracks by Mahalanobis distance inside a gate, nearest first. Three hits confirm a track, and once confirmed it coasts on its prediction for a second when the person is hidden. The prediction handed to the planner extrapolates the velocity and inflates the radius with time, to cover the error in a velocity that was only inferred.</p>
<div class="eq">x = [p_x, p_y, v_x, v_y],   x' = F x,   Q from white acceleration σ_a
gate d² < 9.21;   prediction p(t) = p + v t,   r(t) = r + g t
F = [I  Δt·I;  0  I],   Q = σ_a²·[Δt⁴/4·I  Δt³/2·I;  Δt³/2·I  Δt²·I],   z = p + noise,  H = [I  0],  R = σ_z²·I
d² = ‖z − p̂‖² / (P_pp + σ_z²);   gains  k_p = P_pp / (P_pp + σ_z²),  k_v = P_pv / (P_pp + σ_z²)</div>
<p>Here Δ<i>t</i> is the time since the last scan (0.1 s), <i>z</i> the centre of a detection, <i>p̂</i> the track's predicted position, and <i>P</i><sub>pp</sub>, <i>P</i><sub>pv</sub> the position variance and position–velocity covariance, which both axes share; the update adds <i>k</i><sub>p</sub>(<i>z</i> − <i>p̂</i>) to the position and <i>k</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>z</i> − <i>p̂</i>) to the velocity.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Integrating a random acceleration <i>a</i> over Δ<i>t</i> adds Δ<i>t</i>²/2·<i>a</i> to position and Δ<i>t</i>·<i>a</i> to velocity, so <i>Q</i> = σ<sub>a</sub>² times the outer product of [Δ<i>t</i>²/2, Δ<i>t</i>]. With position-only measurements the Kalman gain settles to the α–β filter\'s steady state, set by one number, the tracking index λ = σ<sub>a</sub>Δ<i>t</i>²/σ<sub>z</sub> (0.08 here): about a third of each innovation goes into position and the velocity error decays with a time constant of about 0.5 s. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/5-tracking-a-constant-velocity-kalman-filter">Derivation: chapter 3 §5</a> · detection: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/4-finding-people-in-a-scan">§4</a> · prediction: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/6-prediction-and-why-the-radius-grows">§6</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Hits are unexplained when the known map has nothing within 0.25 m (or, on a built map, no well-seen occupied cell next to them). Clusters are grown over consecutive beams (0.45 m jump, one beam gap), discarded when wider than 1.2 m, and the centroid is pushed away from the robot because lidar only sees the near side. The tracker runs at the lidar rate, 10 Hz, with σ<sub>z</sub> = 0.1 m and the process noise slider σ<sub>a</sub> (0.8 m/s² by default). Prediction uses a 4 s horizon and inflates the radius by 0.1 m/s. The ST graph in the plot area shows the speed planner\'s view of the predictions along the path.</p>`,
  tries:[['Lidar tracker',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,tab:'per'},
    {setup:'L5, the busy floor: four people at 0.6 to 0.9 m/s, unlabeled beacons, the lidar tracker feeding the speed planner. Perceive tab.',
     see:'Confirmed tracks appear as solid rings with a line to their predicted position and dashed, fading circles one per second ahead; tentative tracks are dotted. The tracker keeps up to six confirmed tracks for four people over the run (a person seen around a corner briefly becomes two), the mean error of the tracked velocity against the true one is about 0.14 m/s, and the speed planner yields four times and escalates to a replan twice. The mission reaches all five goals in about 190 s with two contacts; the closest approach is a 0.55 m overlap, a person walking into the robot from behind a shelf.',
     shows:'Tracks from lidar are good enough to drive by and never clean. The doubles and the velocity error are the price of inferring motion from positions 0.1 s apart; the contacts come from people the lidar could not see until they were already close, which no filter fixes. Compare a track\'s prediction line with the person\'s actual motion when someone turns around: the line follows about half a second later.'}],
   ['Ground truth tracks',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{agents:'truth'},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'The same run with the tracker replaced by the simulator\'s true positions and velocities: no detection, no association, no lag.',
     see:'The velocity error is zero and exactly four tracks exist. The run is not simply better: one contact instead of two, but the robot also gets stuck behind a person who stops in an aisle, replans three times, skips goal 3, and clips a shelf once; four of five goals in 200 s.',
     shows:'The difference between the two runs is what tracking costs, and it is smaller than the difference between two seeds. Perfect tracks make the speed planner\'s decisions sharper, not the floor less crowded; a planner that yields correctly to a person standing in the only aisle is still stuck. Tracking quality is one term in the contact count, and on this level not the dominant one.'}],
   ['Prediction frozen',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{agPred:'static'},tab:'per',plot:'st'},
    {setup:'The lidar tracker again, but the planner is handed each person\'s current position with zero velocity and a constant radius. The ST graph shows the result.',
     see:'The dashed prediction circles collapse onto the track and the prediction line vanishes. The speed planner reacts only once a person is already on the path, so it brakes late: four contacts over the run, twice the tracker\'s, with the same five goals reached. In the ST graph the blocked regions are horizontal bars (a person in one place for the whole horizon) instead of the sloping bands of a moving one.',
     shows:'Prediction is worth exactly the reaction time it buys. Without it the planner is a reflex, and a reflex at 0.8 m/s against people at 0.9 m/s loses. The constant-velocity extrapolation is crude, and crude is enough here: people walk in straight lines for the 4 s that matter.'}]],
  q:[['Why is velocity so much harder than position for this tracker?','Position is measured directly every 0.1 s with 10 cm noise. Velocity is the difference of two such positions divided by 0.1 s: a 10 cm noise becomes a 1 m/s noise, larger than the walking speed. The filter averages that down over many updates, which is where the lag comes from. A sensor that measured velocity (Doppler radar) changes the problem completely.'],
     ['What does raising σ_a do, and what would you set it to for a sprinting child?','It tells the filter that velocity can change a lot between updates, so it trusts recent innovations more: the track follows a turn faster and jitters more in between. For a child, raise it until the lag is acceptable and accept the jitter, or better, grow the predicted radius faster to cover the uncertainty rather than chase it.'],
     ['A track is confirmed after three hits and coasts for one second. Why those numbers?','Three hits at 10 Hz is 0.3 s, long enough that a single stray cluster or a wall corner never becomes a person, short enough that a person stepping out from behind a pillar is tracked before they reach the path. One second of coasting is about one occluding pillar at walking speed; longer and a person who really left keeps blocking the planner.']]},
 {t:'Sensor latency: stale readings, moving prediction',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every sensor on a vehicle delivers the past: exposure, transfer, processing, each adds tens of milliseconds, and a lidar frame can be a tenth of a second old before any software sees it. A filter that applies a reading to the current state, when the reading describes an earlier one, is not using noisy data; it is using correct data at the wrong time, which is worse, because the error is systematic and the filter has no term for it. This is why a timestamp on every reading is the first thing a real perception stack gets right, and why latency is budgeted end to end.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Delay every lidar scan and beacon reading by <i>T</i><sub>s</sub> and deliver it to estimators that believe it is current. The robot has moved <i>v</i>·<i>T</i><sub>s</sub> in the meantime. For a beacon ahead, the reading says "closer than you think" by that much, every time, in the same direction. For the lidar, every hit is placed from the current pose, so the whole scan is shifted backward along the motion. The EKF turns the first into a steady bias, a biased estimate that reports itself as consistent; the map turns the second into walls drawn too close and then erased; the tracker turns it into people who appear to jump between scans.</p>
<div class="eq">reading taken at t, applied at t + T_s;   the innovation carries the motion of T_s:  ν ≈ v·T_s along the beam</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The predicted reading uses the pose at delivery, the actual reading was made from the pose <i>T</i><sub>s</sub> earlier, and for small motion the two poses differ by <i>v</i>·<i>T</i><sub>s</sub> along the heading; project that onto the beam and it is the range innovation. Nothing in <i>S</i> = <i>H</i>Σ<i>H</i>ᵀ + <i>Q</i> accounts for it, so NEES rises exactly as it does for odometry bias. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/7-latency-the-reading-is-true-the-time-is-wrong">Derivation: chapter 3 §7</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The sensor latency slider in the Vehicle tab delays both sensors in whole ticks (0.2 s is four ticks). The estimators are unchanged; nothing is timestamped. The cure, applying a reading at its own time by rewinding the state and replaying the odometry, is described in the chapter and not implemented, so each experiment shows the damage in one consumer.</p>`,
  tries:[['EKF, latency 0.2 s',{sim:true,level:LV_NOISY,set:{loc:'ekf',sensLat:0.2},tab:'veh',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'L3 with the EKF, beacons every 0.2 s delivered 0.2 s late. Vehicle tab, Position error plot.',
     see:'The pose error sits at 12 to 16 cm whenever the robot is driving, against 1 to 5 cm without latency, and the error is almost all along the direction of travel. The filter\'s σ stays at a few centimetres, so NEES reads 50 to 80 and the status strip flags it overconfident for the whole drive; the moment the mission completes and the robot stops, NEES drops to 2.7. All five goals are still reached, in the same time.',
     shows:'The same signature as odometry bias in the localization lessons, from a different cause: a reading that is right about a pose the robot no longer occupies. 0.8 m/s × 0.2 s = 16 cm is the whole effect, and it is invisible to the filter because it never enters any noise model. The mission survives because the error is constant and the planner does not care about 15 cm; a gate would.'}],
   ['Particle filter, latency 0.2 s',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{sensLat:0.2},tab:'veh',plot:'pos'},
    {setup:'L5 with its particle filter, lidar tracker and speed planner, everything delivered 0.2 s late.',
     see:'The pose error is 10 to 18 cm and NEES is in the hundreds. The tracker is where it shows: because every scan is shifted by the robot\'s motion since it was taken, a person\'s hits jump between scans whenever the robot turns, fail the gate, and start new tracks. The tracker creates dozens of confirmed tracks for four people over the run (37 at peak), the speed planner yields to agents numbered in the hundreds, and the mission replans 29 times instead of twice. It still completes, with two contacts.',
     shows:'Latency is not one error, it is one per consumer. The particle filter copes, because its lidar term is tempered and injection repairs what the stale beacons break. The tracker cannot cope, because its gate is built from a 10 cm measurement noise and the latency shift is twice that. Any stack that fuses sensors at different latencies without timestamps builds ghosts.'}],
   ['Mapping with latency 0.3 s',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,set:{sensLat:0.3},tab:'per'},
    {setup:'L4, mapping the building with the EKF, scans delivered 0.3 s late: a shift of 24 cm at cruise speed.',
     see:'Walls perpendicular to the motion are painted about a quarter of a metre too close, then partly erased by the next scan from further on, so they come out thin and broken; 97 % of mapped occupied cells are still near a real obstacle but only 84 % of the seen obstacle cells make it into the map, against 96 % without latency. The planner replans seven times instead of three as walls appear and disappear. Four of four goals.',
     shows:'For a map, latency acts like a pose error that depends on the motion: zero when stopped, largest at speed and in turns. The erase-and-redraw churn is what makes the planner replan twice as often. Timestamping the scan and placing its hits from the pose at scan time fixes it entirely, at the cost of keeping a short history of poses, which every real mapping stack does.'}]],
  q:[['Why does the EKF\'s NEES recover when the robot stops but the map does not?','The EKF\'s latency error is proportional to speed, so it vanishes at rest and the filter is instantly consistent again. The map keeps everything it was told: the walls drawn 24 cm too close are still there until enough free beams erase them, and the walls it failed to draw are still missing.'],
     ['Which is more dangerous: 0.2 s of latency on the beacons or on the lidar?','On this floor, the lidar: the beacons bias the pose by 16 cm, which the planner tolerates, while the lidar shift breaks the people tracker\'s association and the planner then reacts to ghosts. In general, whichever sensor feeds the consumer with the tightest gate.'],
     ['What would a lagged-state filter need to store?','The pose estimate (or the odometry increments) for at least the maximum latency, so that a reading stamped at t − T_s can be applied to the state at t − T_s and the increments since then replayed. At 20 Hz and 0.3 s that is six poses; the cost is trivial, the discipline of timestamping everything is the real work.']]},
 {t:'People who react',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A planner is evaluated against a model of the people around it, and the model can flatter it. Scripted pedestrians walk their line whatever the robot does, so every near miss is the robot\'s fault; real people step aside, slow down, and absorb a good share of a robot\'s mistakes. A benchmark against people who yield reports fewer contacts than the planner earned. This lesson puts a reactive pedestrian model next to the scripted one so that the difference can be measured, and so that the metrics can be read with the right suspicion.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Social forces treat each person as a particle driven toward their goal and pushed away from everything near. The drive is a relaxation toward the preferred velocity; the pushes are exponentials of the overlap, negligible beyond a few tens of centimetres and overwhelming on contact, so the crowd behaves like a set of soft bodies. The robot is one of the things pushing, with a longer reach than a person, because people give machines room. Walls push along the gradient of the distance field, which the costmap of lesson 2 already provides.</p>
<div class="eq">F = (v₀ e − v)/τ + Σ A·exp((r_i + r_j − d)/B)·n + walls,   τ = 0.5 s
robot:  A_r·exp((r_i + r_robot − d)/B_r)·n        walls:  A_w·exp((r_i − D)/B_w)·∇D
v ← v + F·Δt,  |v| ≤ 1.3 v₀;    p ← p + v·Δt   (every 50 ms tick)</div>
<p>Here <i>e</i> is the unit vector toward the person's goal, <i>v</i> their velocity, <i>d</i> the distance between centres and <i>n</i> the unit vector from the other body to the person; the robot counts with <i>r</i><sub>robot</sub> = 0.3 m (0.55 m for the car), and <i>D</i> is the distance from the person's centre to the nearest wall, read from the map's distance field.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> It is a model, not a derivation: Helbing and Molnár (1995) proposed the drive-plus-repulsion form because it reproduces lane formation and bottleneck behaviour in crowds, and it has been the standard pedestrian model in robotics benchmarks since. The constants here (people <i>A</i> = 2, <i>B</i> = 0.35 m; robot <i>A</i> = 3, <i>B</i> = 0.5 m; walls <i>A</i> = 2.5, <i>B</i> = 0.25 m; speed capped at 1.3 <i>v</i>₀) are tuned to look right on this floor, not fitted to data. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch3/8-people-who-react-the-social-force-model">Derivation: chapter 3 §8</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The People switch is in the Vehicle tab. Scripted people walk back and forth on their segment at 0.6 to 0.9 m/s and stop briefly at each end. Reactive people have the same segments and speeds but move by the forces above, integrated every tick. The Response to agents selector in the Control tab chooses whether the robot plans its speed against the tracked people (the ST planner) or ignores them. Contacts and the closest approach are in the Telemetry metrics; a contact also pauses the run unless "Pause on contact" is off.</p>`,
  tries:[['Reactive people, speed planning',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{people:'reactive'},tab:'veh'},
    {setup:'L5 with reactive people; the robot tracks them and plans its speed against the predictions.',
     see:'Zero contacts over the run, closest approach 0.18 m, five goals. Watch a person meet the robot in an aisle: they drift sideways a step before the robot has slowed, and the robot\'s yield is shorter than with scripted people. Four replans, all "agent blocking".',
     shows:'Both sides are avoiding; the result is better than either would manage alone. This run is the flattering benchmark: the zero depends on the people\'s cooperation as much as on the planner.'}],
   ['Reactive people, ignore',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{people:'reactive',spd:'none'},tab:'ctl'},
    {setup:'Reactive people again, with the response to agents off: the robot drives its path at cruise speed as if the floor were empty.',
     see:'Two contacts and a closest approach of −0.18 m (an overlap), with the same five goals. Most encounters end with the person stepping out of the way; the contacts are the two where they could not, in a narrow aisle and from behind.',
     shows:'A robot that does nothing about people scores only two contacts on this floor, which looks almost acceptable and is entirely the people\'s doing. The number measures the crowd\'s politeness, not the planner.'}],
   ['Scripted people, ignore',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{spd:'none'},tab:'ctl'},
    {setup:'Scripted people, who never deviate, and the response to agents off.',
     see:'Four contacts and a closest approach of −0.57 m: the robot drives through people. With the speed planner on (the level\'s default) the same scripted people produce two contacts.',
     shows:'Against people who do not yield, the planner\'s contribution is visible: four contacts to two. Against people who do, it was zero to two. Same planner, same floor; the model of the people changes the measured value of the planner by a factor of two. Report both, and when only one can be run, run the stricter one.'}]],
  q:[['Which metric is harder to flatter: contact count or closest approach?','Closest approach, slightly: a yielding person who steps aside late still produces a small distance, where the contact count would show nothing. Neither is immune. The honest comparison is the same planner against both people models, and the difference between them is how much the planner relies on cooperation.'],
     ['The repulsion from the robot has a longer reach (B = 0.5 m) than from another person (0.35 m). What does that assumption do to the results?','It makes the reactive people give the robot more room than they give each other, which lowers contacts for every planner, including the one that ignores them. Shrink it to 0.35 and the "ignore" run collects more contacts; the ranking of planners is probably unchanged, their absolute scores are not. Any constant in a pedestrian model is also a constant in your benchmark.'],
     ['Why cap the speed at 1.3 v₀?','Without a cap the exponential pushes can fling a person at several metres per second when two bodies overlap, which happens at every near miss. The cap keeps the model in the range of a brisk walk, which is what people actually do when they sidestep.']]}],
plan:[
 {t:'A*: the heuristic and ε',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Graph search is the workhorse of robot planning: a costmap is a graph, a route is a path through it, and A* finds the cheapest one with a guarantee. The guarantee is the point. A planner that is usually fine and occasionally sends the robot the long way round is hard to certify; one with a bound on how far from optimal it can be, even a loose bound, can be reasoned about. The heuristic weight ε is the single knob that trades that bound for speed, and understanding exactly what it buys and costs is the difference between tuning and guessing.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> A* keeps a queue of cells, each a node of the graph, ordered by <i>f</i> = <i>g</i> + <i>h</i>: the cost already paid to reach the cell plus an estimate of the cost still to go. It always expands the cell that looks cheapest in total, so it heads toward the goal without ignoring cheaper detours. If the estimate <i>h</i> never exceeds the true remaining cost (it is admissible), the first path to reach the goal is optimal: any cheaper path would have had a cell in the queue with a smaller <i>f</i>, which would have been expanded first. On an 8-connected grid the octile distance is the exact free-space cost, so it is admissible and as tight as a free-space heuristic can be.</p>
<div class="eq">f = g + ε·h,   ε = 1: optimal;   ε > 1: cost ≤ ε · optimal, far fewer expansions
h = (max(Δi, Δj) + (√2 − 1)·min(Δi, Δj)) · 0.1 m     Δi, Δj: cells to the goal in x and y
g += length · (1 + 3 · penalty of the cell entered),   length 0.1 m or 0.1√2 m</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Run the optimality argument with <i>g</i> + ε<i>h</i>: any cell on the optimal path still in the queue has <i>f</i> ≤ ε·<i>C</i>*, and the goal was popped with <i>f</i> no larger than that, so the path found costs at most ε times the optimum. The bound is tight only in adversarial mazes; on a floor with aisles the greedy direction is usually right and the path is within a few percent. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/2-a-best-first-search-with-a-promise">Derivation: chapter 4 §2</a> · the graph and its costs: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/1-the-costmap-as-a-graph">§1</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Cells are 0.1 m, moves go to eight neighbours (no cutting between two touching obstacles), and the cost of a move is its length times (1 + 3 × inflation penalty) of the cell it enters, so the planner minimizes cost, not distance. The cell sequence is reduced to turning points and shortcut with straight segments that keep 0.5 m from obstacles. The search layer shows the closed set, the cells A* expanded, and the event log reports path length, expansions and planning time for every plan.</p>`,
  tries:[['A*, ε = 1',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{eps:1},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'L2, the warehouse with a known map, A* with ε = 1 and the search layer on. Plan tab.',
     see:'Four legs: 8.6, 13.0, 12.9 and 26.1 m, expanding 1 258, 3 199, 1 105 and 8 615 cells in 1 to 14 ms each; the mission completes in 78 s. The expansion cloud fills the aisles around the direct route and spills into side aisles that might have been shorter, which is what proving optimality costs.',
     shows:'Optimal here means cheapest in the costmap\'s currency, which charges extra inside the inflation band. The 13.0 m second leg is the cheapest path, not the shortest; the next experiment finds a shorter one that costs more. Every cell expanded outside the final path is the price of the guarantee: A* has to rule out every alternative that could have been cheaper.'}],
   ['A*, ε = 3',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{eps:3},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'The same level with the heuristic weighted by 3: the search is greedy toward the goal.',
     see:'The same four goals in 78 s, with legs of 8.5, 12.5, 12.9 and 26.1 m: within a few percent of the ε = 1 paths and one of them shorter in metres. The expansion counts fall to 1 117, 1 139, 118 and 626, up to 14 times fewer on the longer legs, and the cloud narrows to a corridor along the route.',
     shows:'On a floor with aisles the greedy direction is almost always right, so weighted A* loses almost nothing. The bound says the path could be three times longer; the measurement says a few percent. The bound matters in mazes, where the direction the heuristic likes is a dead end, and there weighted A* really does find bad paths fast. Know which kind of map you have.'}],
   ['Straight line (no planner)',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{plan:'straight'},tab:'plan'},
    {setup:'The planner replaced by a straight segment from the estimate to the goal, which ignores the costmap entirely.',
     see:'The robot drives into the first shelf within a few seconds, is reported stuck three times at five-second intervals, skips the goal, and repeats for the next: three goals skipped in 50 s, one collision, 9 m of driving. The tracker is doing its job perfectly on a path that goes through a wall.',
     shows:'What the planner is for, stated by its absence. Every layer below the planner assumes the path is feasible; the tracker has no way to know it is heading into a shelf and no authority to do anything about it if it did. The mission layer eventually gives up on each goal, which is the only thing that keeps the run from lasting forever.'}]],
  q:[['The octile heuristic ignores the inflation penalty. Is it still admissible?','Yes. The penalty only adds to the true cost of a move, so the free-space distance is a lower bound on the costmap cost as well. Ignoring it makes the heuristic looser, which costs expansions, not optimality. A heuristic that tried to include the penalty would have to know the path to compute it.'],
     ['Why does ε = 3 sometimes return a path shorter in metres than ε = 1?','Because ε = 1 minimizes cost, and cost includes the inflation surcharge. The 12.5 m path at ε = 3 passes through the band where the 13.0 m path at ε = 1 goes around it; the shorter path costs more in the costmap\'s terms. "Optimal" is always with respect to the cost you defined, and the inflation band is part of that definition.'],
     ['A* expands 8 615 cells on the 26 m leg. The grid has 38 400. When would it expand all of them?','When the goal is unreachable: A* has to exhaust every reachable cell before it can say there is no path. On an unknown map with unknown-as-lethal, that is what happens three times per goal in the costmap lesson. Real stacks bound the search or check reachability first.']]},
 {t:'RRT*: sampling, rewiring, and luck',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Grids stop working when the state has more than a few dimensions: a 7-joint arm, a car with heading and speed, a drone. Sampling-based planners replaced them there, and RRT* is the one with a proof: it is asymptotically optimal, meaning that as the number of samples grows without limit its path converges to the optimum. What the proof does not say, and what this lesson shows, is how far from the optimum a finite budget leaves you, and how much of the answer is luck.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Grow a tree from the start. Each iteration draws a random point (the goal itself one time in ten), steps 0.6 m from the nearest tree node toward it, and, if the step is collision-free, connects the new node to the cheapest nearby parent rather than simply the nearest. Then it rewires: any neighbour that would be cheaper to reach through the new node is re-parented to it. The neighbourhood shrinks as the tree grows, so the work per iteration stays logarithmic while the tree keeps improving. After the budget is spent, the best node within a metre of the goal is connected to it and the path is read back through the parents.</p>
<div class="eq">r = min(8 √(log n / n), 1.5 m)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The proof of asymptotic optimality needs the neighbourhood radius to shrink no faster than γ√(log <i>n</i>/<i>n</i>) with γ above a constant set by the free area; then the expected number of neighbours grows like log <i>n</i>, enough for rewiring to keep finding improvements, and the cost converges to the optimum with probability one. The lab\'s γ = 8 is below the theoretical constant for this floor (about 22), which is common practice: the condition is sufficient, not necessary, and a theoretical γ would make every iteration examine most of the tree. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/3-rrt-planning-by-sampling">Derivation: chapter 4 §3</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The sample budget is the Plan tab\'s RRT* samples slider (300 to 4000, 1500 by default). Segments are checked against the costmap at 0.45 m clearance (lethal plus 0.05). The search layer draws the tree; the event log reports the path length, the number of nodes that made it into the tree (rejected samples do not), and the time. The random stream is seeded, so the same seed gives the same tree, and the Next seed button advances it.</p>`,
  tries:[['RRT*',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{plan:'rrtstar'},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'L2 with RRT* at 1500 samples and the default seed; search layer on.',
     see:'A tree of about 870 nodes fans out over the floor in 10 to 20 ms, and the first leg comes out at 8.3 m, next to A*\'s 8.6 m. The later legs are 12.7, 17.7 and 25.5 m against A*\'s 13.0, 12.9 and 26.1; the third leg is 37 % longer because the tree committed to the far aisle. The mission completes in 81 s against 78 s for A*.',
     shows:'Sampling found the near-optimal route three times out of four and a clearly worse one once, in the same run, with the same settings. The path through the tree is only as good as the samples that happened to fall in the right aisle early; rewiring improves connections inside the radius, but it cannot move a subtree to a different aisle once the tree has grown past it.'}],
   ['RRT*, next seed',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,seed:'next',set:{plan:'rrtstar'},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'The same planner and budget with the next random seed.',
     see:'The first leg is 8.8 m, the second 19.8 m (A*: 13.0), the third 18.8 m (A*: 12.9), and the mission takes 90 s instead of 81. Across ten seeds the first leg alone ranges from 8.3 to 15.3 m: half the seeds find the near aisle, half take the long way round.',
     shows:'This is the variance the grid planner does not have. Two runs that differ only in a random seed differ by 9 s of mission time and by 50 % on individual legs. Benchmarking a sampling planner on one seed measures the seed; the honest number is a distribution over seeds, which the Experiments tab can produce.'}],
   ['RRT*, 4000 samples',{sim:true,level:LV_WAREHOUSE,set:{plan:'rrtstar',rrtIt:4000},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'The budget raised to 4000 samples (about 2300 tree nodes), default seed.',
     see:'The tree is denser and the legs are 8.3, 13.8, 18.3 and 25.8 m, mission 84 s: better than 1500 on this seed, still a long third leg. Across ten seeds the first leg finds the short route eight times instead of five; at 300 samples it finds no connection to the goal at all four times in ten.',
     shows:'More samples shift the distribution, slowly: convergence is in the limit, and the limit is far. The cost per iteration grows with the tree, so 4000 samples take about three times as long as 1500 for a modest gain in reliability. On a 2-D floor with a known map, a grid planner is simply the better tool; RRT* earns its place where no grid is possible, and the variance comes with it.'}]],
  q:[['Why does the planner choose the cheapest parent in a radius rather than the nearest node?','Connecting to the nearest node is RRT, which converges to a feasible path but not to the optimal one; its paths wander. Choosing the parent that gives the lowest cost-to-come, and then rewiring the neighbours, is what makes RRT* improve the tree as it grows. Without the rewiring step the tree never corrects an early bad connection.'],
     ['The radius formula shrinks with n. What goes wrong if it is held fixed at 1.5 m?','Nothing in the limit, but every iteration then has to examine every node within 1.5 m, which grows linearly with the tree, so the cost per iteration grows with n instead of with log n. The shrinking radius is the compromise that keeps enough neighbours to converge and few enough to stay fast.'],
     ['Ten seeds give first legs between 8.3 and 15.3 m. What single number would you report?','Not the mean. The median and the fraction of seeds within 10 % of the grid optimum say what a user will experience; the worst case says what a safety argument needs. Any one of them is better than the number from the seed you happened to run.']]},
 {t:'Hybrid A*: planning for a car',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A car cannot move sideways and cannot turn in place. Its heading is part of its state, and from any heading only poses along arcs of at least the minimum turning radius are reachable. A grid path, with its sharp corners and no heading, is not a route a car can follow, and parking, which is nothing but arriving at a heading, is impossible on one. Hybrid A* is the planner that made automated parking and low-speed manoeuvring practical, and it does so by keeping the search discrete while making every edge a real car motion.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Search over cells as before, but let each node carry an exact pose, and let each edge be a short arc the car can drive: forward or in reverse, at one of five steering angles. Two cells count as the same node only if their headings also match to within 5°, so the search can hold several headings per place. Two heuristics guide it, and the search uses whichever is larger: a grid distance from the goal that knows about obstacles but not about turning, and the Reeds–Shepp length to the goal pose that knows about turning but not about obstacles. Near the goal the planner stops searching and tries to shoot straight to the goal pose with a Reeds–Shepp curve; if the shot is collision-free, the plan is done.</p>
<div class="eq">cells 0.2 m × 5°,  5 steering angles × {forward, reverse}, 0.4 m arcs
h = ε · max(2D Dijkstra with obstacles, Reeds–Shepp without obstacles)
analytic expansion: try a Reeds–Shepp shot to the goal, accept it if collision-free</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Each heuristic underestimates the true cost, so each is admissible, and the maximum of two admissible heuristics is admissible and tighter than either. Reeds and Shepp proved that the shortest obstacle-free path for a car that can reverse is one of 48 words of arcs and straights; by symmetry that is 12 families, which the lab evaluates in closed form for every query. The three-circle footprint over-approximates the car\'s rectangle by about 0.14 m at the corners, on the safe side. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/4-planning-for-a-car">Derivation: chapter 4 §4</a></p>
<p><b>The primitives.</b> Each edge is the kinematic bicycle, pose at the rear axle and θ̇ = <i>v</i> tan δ/<i>L</i>, driven for a signed distance <i>s</i> = ±0.4 m at a fixed steering angle, which is an arc of curvature κ = tan δ/<i>L</i>. It is integrated exactly in four 0.1 m steps, with the footprint checked after each; δ<sub>p</sub> is the largest angle the planner uses, 85 % of the lock, and the edge cost writes out the preferences described below.</p>
<div class="eq">x' = x + (sin(θ + κs) − sin θ)/κ,   y' = y − (cos(θ + κs) − cos θ)/κ,   θ' = θ + κs     (κ = 0: straight)
κ = tan δ / L,   δ ∈ {−δ_p, −δ_p/2, 0, δ_p/2, δ_p},   s = +0.4 m forward, −0.4 m reverse
edge cost = 0.4 (× 2 in reverse) + 0.05|δ|/δ_p + 0.1|δ − δ_prev|/δ_p + 2 per gear change + 0.8·prox²
prox = max(0, (0.4 − clearance)/0.4)</div>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> L6 is the parking lot with four posed goals. Wheelbase 0.6 m and a steering limit of 32°, so the car\'s true minimum radius is 0.96 m; the planner keeps 15 % in reserve and plans at 85 % of the lock, a 1.17 m radius, so the tracker has steering authority left to correct with. Reverse costs double, a gear change costs 2, and a proximity term keeps the car off walls. The budget is 60 000 expansions. The event log reports length, expansions, cusps (gear changes) and whether a Reeds–Shepp shot finished the plan.</p>`,
  tries:[['Hybrid A*',{sim:true,level:5,tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'L6 defaults: Hybrid A* with both heuristics, 85 % of the lock, reverse allowed. Search layer on.',
     see:'Four bays in 150 s, no collisions, each arrival within 0.1 m and 5° of the posed goal. The four plans take 6 950, 10 230, 30 and 162 expansions (185, 156, 24 and 23 ms); three end with a Reeds–Shepp shot. The third plan is the one to watch: 30 expansions, then a shot that parks the car with two cusps. The search layer shows the expanded poses as a scatter that fills the lot for the long plans and barely exists for the short ones.',
     shows:'The analytic expansion is what makes the planner fast: the search only has to get the car into a room from which a shot is clear, and the closed-form curve does the parking. The 85 % margin is invisible here because it works; the third experiment removes it. The heading tolerance at arrival is 0.2 rad, and the plans use most of it, because 5° heading cells cannot represent anything finer.'}],
   ['2D heuristic only',{sim:true,level:5,set:{hybH:'2d'},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'The Reeds–Shepp term removed from the heuristic; the search is guided by grid distance alone.',
     see:'The first plan is found faster (1 515 expansions, 17.1 m) but arrives 11° off instead of 5°: the grid distance says nothing about heading, so the search settles the heading in the last metre and takes whatever it can get. The run then meets the lot\'s pedestrians differently, with three agent-blocking replans and four contacts, and finishes the four bays in 180 s.',
     shows:'A heuristic that is blind to part of the state is admissible and still misleading: it steers the search to the right place at the wrong heading, and a car cannot fix its heading on the spot. The contacts in this run are the lot\'s people crossing a different route, not the heuristic\'s fault directly, which is a reminder that a single run compares everything that changed, not only the thing you meant to change.'}],
   ['Planner at 100 % steering',{sim:true,level:5,set:{planPct:100},tab:'plan'},
    {setup:'The planner allowed the full 32° lock, turning radius 0.96 m instead of 1.17 m.',
     see:'The plans are shorter and tighter, and the car gets stuck twice: at 40 s and 125 s it stops making progress on a full-lock arc, the mission replans from a standstill, and it scrapes an obstacle twice on the way (two collisions, closest clearance 0.12 m). The four bays are still reached, in 135 s.',
     shows:'A path planned at the vehicle\'s limit cannot be tracked. On a full-lock arc the tracker\'s correction is to steer more, and there is no more; any error, from a 5° heading cell or a wheel slipping, is uncorrectable until the arc ends. The 15 % reserve is the planner leaving the tracker something to work with, and it is the first place planning and control meet.'}],
   ['Plain A* on the car',{sim:true,level:5,set:{plan:'astar'},tab:'plan'},
    {setup:'The grid planner on the car: a path of straight segments and sharp corners, with no heading at the goal.',
     see:'The car follows the first segment, swings wide at the first corner, and is stuck within 35 s. Each goal is skipped after three stuck replans; all four are skipped by 91 s with 15 m driven and one collision. Nothing was parked.',
     shows:'The grid path is not wrong, it is unreachable: no sequence of arcs of radius 1.17 m follows a 90° corner of radius zero, and the bays require a heading the path does not carry. Everything Hybrid A* adds, the heading in the state, the arc primitives, the Reeds–Shepp shot, exists to make the path something this vehicle can actually execute.'}]],
  q:[['Why is the maximum of the two heuristics admissible, and why not their sum?','Each heuristic is a lower bound on the true cost, so the larger one is still a lower bound and the search is still optimal. The sum would double-count: a path that is long because of obstacles and long because of turning is not twice as long. The sum would be inadmissible and could make the search skip the optimal path.'],
     ['The search closes poses in 0.2 m × 5° cells. What does a finer heading resolution buy and cost?','Finer cells let the search represent more headings per place, so arrival headings are more accurate and fewer feasible routes are discarded as duplicates; the cost is a larger state space (72 headings now, 360 at 1°) and a larger closed set to search before the shot lands. The analytic expansion is what makes 5° enough: the shot is continuous and fixes the last few degrees.'],
     ['Reverse costs double and a gear change costs 2. What would change if reverse were free?','The planner would reverse whenever it saved a few centimetres, producing plans with many cusps, each a stop and a gear change that the tracker executes slowly and the passengers dislike. The penalties encode a preference for forward, smooth motion; set them to zero and the search still finds feasible paths, just unpleasant ones.']]},
 {t:'Replanning and the behaviour layer',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A plan is a promise built on assumptions: the map is right, the estimate is right, the people will be where they were predicted, the robot will make progress. Every one of those breaks, routinely. The behaviour layer is the small piece of logic that notices and decides what to do: plan again, wait, give up on this goal. It is tiny compared with the planners it supervises and it determines most of what a user sees the robot do. The Mission tab draws it live because it is the part of the stack most worth watching.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Plans are cheap, milliseconds on this grid, so the policy is to replan whenever an assumption behind the current plan breaks rather than to make the plan robust to everything. The machine has a handful of states, PLAN, DRIVE, NO_PATH, LOST and DONE, and a set of triggers that move between them: a lethal cell on the path ahead, the estimate too far from the path, no progress in five seconds, a timer, a report from the speed planner that a person cannot be got past by slowing. Each trigger carries a reason, which the event log prints, so the log is a trace of which assumptions broke and when.</p>
<div class="eq">DRIVE → PLAN on: path blocked · off path · stuck · periodic · agent blocking
PLAN → NO_PATH → (2 s) PLAN, skip after 3</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> There is nothing to derive; it is a design, and the design choices are the thresholds: a 6 m look-ahead on the path checked every 0.5 s, 1 m off the path, 0.15 m of progress in 5 s, three failures. The one trigger with a mechanism behind it is "agent blocking": for that single plan, the people\'s predicted discs over the next 2 s are stamped into the costmap as lethal, so the new route avoids where they will be, and the costmap is rebuilt clean afterwards. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/5-the-mission-layer">The machine, the thresholds and goal ordering: chapter 4 §5</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The Mission tab draws the state machine with the current state lit and the last transition highlighted; the event log carries each transition\'s reason. Goal ordering (in order, greedy, optimal) is decided once at mission start from costmap distances. The periodic replan slider adds a timer trigger; its replans are not logged, to keep the log readable, and they pre-empt the other triggers. A reached goal gets a tick on the map; a skipped one stays greyed without it, and its hover card says skipped.</p>`,
  tries:[['Discover the building',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,tab:'mis'},
    {setup:'L4, the unknown building, mapping as it drives, no periodic replan. Mission tab.',
     see:'Three "Replan (path blocked)" events at 4.3, 13.2 and 31.2 s, each a wall that appeared across the planned route as the lidar reached it, and four fresh plans at the goals. Every replan is from a few hundred to a few thousand expansions and under 10 ms. All four goals in 67 s.',
     shows:'Optimistic planning plus cheap replanning is a complete strategy for an unknown map: plan through the unknown as if it were free, and correct the moment the map disagrees. Watch the state machine flicker DRIVE → PLAN → DRIVE at each wall; the robot barely slows, because the plan is back before the next tick.'}],
   ['Periodic replan every 1 s',{sim:true,level:LV_BUILDING,set:{replan:1},tab:'mis'},
    {setup:'The same level with a 1 s periodic replan.',
     see:'The log shows no "path blocked" replans at all, because the periodic plan, which is silent, always arrives first; about seventy plans run during the mission. The route is the same and the mission takes 70 s instead of 67: the plan is rebuilt from the current estimate every second, and each rebuild restarts the path slightly differently under the tracker.',
     shows:'Replanning on a timer is the simple way to keep a plan fresh and it is almost free here, about 3 % of the mission time. On a real vehicle the cost is different: each new plan can differ from the last by a lane\'s width, and a tracker following a path that keeps moving oscillates. Event-triggered replanning changes the plan only when something changed; periodic replanning changes it whether or not anything did.'}],
   ['Agent-blocking replans',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,tab:'mis'},
    {setup:'L5, the busy floor, with the speed planner reporting people it cannot get past. Mission tab.',
     see:'The speed planner yields four times over the run, and twice, at 51 s and 99 s, it escalates: "Replan (agent blocking)", and the new path, 13.6 m and 16.7 m, goes around the person\'s predicted positions instead of waiting behind them. Five goals in 121 s with two contacts, both from people stepping out behind shelves.',
     shows:'The layered design: the trajectory layer (speed) handles most people by slowing, and only when slowing cannot resolve the conflict does the route change. Each escalation costs a full replan with the people stamped into the costmap for 2 s of prediction, cheap here, and the result is a route that is right if the prediction was. The two contacts show the limit: a person the lidar had not seen is in no prediction and in no costmap.'}]],
  q:[['Why skip a goal after three failures instead of trying forever?','A goal that cannot be reached, because it is walled off, in unknown space treated as lethal, or occupied by someone who will not move, would otherwise stall the whole mission. Three attempts over about 6 s is enough to tell a transient blockage from a permanent one on this floor; the right number depends on how often the world changes.'],
     ['The "off path" trigger fires when the estimate is 1 m from the path. What happens if the estimate is wrong?','The robot replans from a pose it is not at, drives the new path from where it actually is, and fires the trigger again, which is what the kidnapped-EKF run in the localization lessons shows: a loop of off-path replans around a phantom pose. The behaviour layer can only act on the estimate; recovering the estimate is the estimator\'s job, and the lost guard is the hook that lets it ask for time.'],
     ['Optimal goal ordering is solved exactly here. Why can that not scale?','It enumerates orders: 8 goals is 40 320, 15 goals is over a trillion. Branch-and-bound prunes most of them but not enough. Real fleets use heuristics (nearest neighbour, 2-opt improvements) and accept a few percent of extra distance for an answer in milliseconds.']]},
 {t:'Space-time A*: planning with time as a coordinate',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A route through a crowd is not a line, it is a line with a schedule. The same doorway is free now and blocked in two seconds; the right plan may be to wait, which a spatial planner cannot even express. Adding time to the search state is the simplest way to plan with predictions, and it exposes the question that every planner around people has to answer: how far ahead can a prediction be trusted, and what should the plan assume beyond that?</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The state is a cell and a time step. Moving to a neighbour takes the time to cross a cell at the reference speed; waiting is a move that takes one step and goes nowhere. A cell is blocked at a time when a predicted person covers it then, so the obstacles are different in every time slice and a corridor can be free at step 5 and blocked at step 8. Beyond the prediction horizon all times collapse into one and the search is plain A* on the static map. The result is a path with waits folded in; the speed planner keeps the schedule and reacts to prediction error.</p>
<div class="eq">state (i, j, k),   k = t / Δt,   Δt = cell / v₀
blocked(i, j, k) if ‖c_ij − p_a(kΔt)‖ < r_a(kΔt) + r_robot + margin for any agent a
p_a(t) = p_a + v_a·t,   r_a(t) = r_a + g·t   (the tracker's constant-velocity prediction)
move cost = Δt·(1 or √2) + 0.3·Δt·penalty,   wait = 1.2·Δt;   h = octile distance in cells · Δt</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The search is A* with an extra coordinate; its heuristic, the octile distance in time units, assumes free space and no waiting, so it is admissible and the path is optimal in time on the discretized problem. The blocked test is the predicted disc of the tracking lesson, grown by the robot\'s radius and a clearance margin; diagonal moves are checked at their midpoint step as well, so a two-step move cannot pass through a person. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch4/6-space-time-a-time-as-a-coordinate">Derivation: chapter 4 §6</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Cells are 0.2 m (coarser than the spatial grid, to pay for the time dimension), the time step is 0.2 m divided by the cruise speed, 0.25 s at 0.8 m/s, and the horizon is the prediction horizon slider, 4 s by default. Waiting costs 1.2 times a move so the planner waits only when it must. The search is capped at 60 000 expansions. The ST graph in the plot area shows the speed planner\'s view of the same predictions along the chosen path.</p>`,
  tries:[['Space-time A*',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{plan:'stastar'},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'L5, the busy floor, with the space-time planner and the search layer on.',
     see:'Seven plans with 200 to 3 500 expansions each, fewer than plain A* on the same legs because the cells are twice as large. Once, at 106 s, the planner escalates with an agent-blocking replan and then reports "no space-time route within the horizon"; the mission retries two seconds later and continues. Five goals in 128 s with two contacts, the same two as A* with the agent layer.',
     shows:'On this floor the contacts come from people stepping out from behind shelves inside the planner\'s reaction time, and no planner that works from predictions can see them. Space-time A* changes the route where the route is the problem, in doorways and narrow aisles with a person coming the other way; here the speed planner was already resolving most encounters by slowing, so the gain is small. The failure to find a route within the horizon is honest: the horizon was full.'}],
   ['A* with the agent layer',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{plan:'astar'},tab:'plan',layers:{search:true}},
    {setup:'Plain A* on the same floor; people enter the costmap only when the speed planner escalates.',
     see:'Five plans of 1 400 to 5 900 expansions, two agent-blocking replans, five goals in 121 s with two contacts. The route is spatial only; every encounter is handled by the speed planner until it gives up and asks for a replan.',
     shows:'Faster mission, same contacts: the comparison that keeps a new planner honest. A richer planner is worth its complexity only where the simpler one fails, and this floor, with wide aisles and few people, is not that place. Narrow the aisles or add people and the ranking changes; the lab cannot do that yet, which is a known limit.'}],
   ['Space-time A*, reactive people',{sim:true,level:LV_BUSY,set:{plan:'stastar',people:'reactive'},tab:'plan'},
    {setup:'The space-time planner against people who step aside.',
     see:'No contacts, closest approach 0.43 m, five goals in 132 s. Around 73 s the particle filter briefly loses the robot and the mission replans six times in two seconds as the estimate settles ("relocalized"), then carries on.',
     shows:'The combination that looks best is the one with the most help: predictions the planner can trust plus people who absorb the error. The relocalization episode is unrelated to the planner and shows in every run on this level with a different timing; a run is a sum of everything in the stack, and the planner is one term.'}]],
  q:[['Why are the space-time cells 0.2 m when the spatial grid is 0.1 m?','Adding a time axis multiplies the state space by the number of time steps (17 at the default horizon), so the grid is coarsened to keep the search tractable: 0.2 m cells have a quarter of the cells, and the budget of 60 000 expansions stays enough. The price is a coarser route that the tracker smooths.'],
     ['What should the planner assume about people beyond the prediction horizon?','That they are not there, which is what the lab does by collapsing time after the horizon, or that they are everywhere they could reach, which would block most of the map. Neither is right; the honest answer is a growing uncertainty the planner treats as a soft cost, which is what the speed planner\'s growing radius approximates and the space-time search does not.'],
     ['Waiting costs 1.2 times a move. What happens at 1.0 and at 3.0?','At 1.0 waiting and moving are equal, and the planner may wait in place even when a detour of the same length would have made progress toward the goal; ties resolve arbitrarily. At 3.0 the planner detours around people it could have waited for, taking longer routes to avoid a one-second pause. The factor encodes how much the mission values progress over patience.']]}],
};
